  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Selectors Discovered**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the first selector, focusing on how it relates to the `<BrandMark />` component. Currently, I\'m examining how its specific structure and nesting correspond to this SVG\'s properties and potential styling, specifically the `<rect>`. There\'s nothing in that CSS block, but the selector shows how it is accessed.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Key SVG Placement**\n\nI\'ve located another instance of the `<BrandMark />` component, this time within the `Footer.tsx` file. The second selector clearly pinpoints the SVG icon, specifically a `<path>` element nested within it. This SVG is the only one in the `<BrandMark inverted />` component, and it is rendered within both `Navbar.tsx` and `Footer.tsx`. I need to consider why direct referencing is desired, and how it will impact the styling and overall layout, especially with that media attachment.\n\n\n**Evaluating Color Contrast Adjustments**\n\nI\'m now diving into the color contrast, specifically for the teal logo on white versus dark navy backgrounds. The teal `#0F766E` offers a nice contrast against white, but on the dark navy, it\'s quite low. My current strategy is to find the best way to make the teal logo pop on a dark navy background. I\'m exploring various options, like a white container for the image, or CSS filters to boost brightness or contrast. I want to see what delivers the cleanest look!\n\n\n**Testing Color Transformations**\n\nI\'ve tested the `brightness-[1.65] saturate-[1.1]` filter with the `inverted` prop, which brightens the teal logo to a luminous teal. It looks clean and bright without extra boxes, offering a great visual pop. I will compare it against the logo in a dark/white frame. I am also checking whether the `/public` directory is correctly served in both dev and production environments. I can see that Vite handles this automatically via `vite.middlewares` in dev, and `express.static(distDir)` in prod. Next, I\'ll update `/packages/ui/src/components/BrandMark.tsx`.\n\n\n"
