    thinking: "**Clarifying The Audit**\n\nI\'m seeing a much clearer picture thanks to this audit, especially regarding all the PDF spec codes. It seems like the audit has brought to light all the relevant internal PDF spec codes (`METR-SPEC-001`, `CRM-01`, `SAL-01`, `FIN-01`, `Section A6 & A7`, `Section B1\342\200\223B9`, `Section C7`, `Section D1 & D2`, `DEC-04`, `UAT-04`, `UAT-05`). It is also clear that the audit highlights the importance of keeping all spec codes up-to-date.\n\n\n**Mapping Technical Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on translating the technical aspects into business-friendly terms, especially concerning the transformation\'s impact. The goal is a cohesive representation that retains the visual design and user experience, which is really important. I\'ve begun to tackle this section-by-section, starting with the Navbar. I\'m aiming to make sure we replace the internal codes, such as the `Navbar.tsx` reference, with business-focused language, such as changing `Architecture` to `Security & Reliability` (or variations thereof).\n\n\n**Refining Hero Section**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the Hero Section, aiming for maximum clarity and impact. I\'ve streamlined the eyebrow text and secondary CTA. I\'m excited about the new proof strip, and I\'m updating the interactive workspace preview with more business-friendly phrasing. For instance, `15 PostgreSQL Schemas` becomes simply `Connected Business Modules`.\n\n\n**Updating Ledger Elements**\n\nI\'m currently updating the \'Ledger Balance \316\224\' section with refined language. Specifically, I\'m changing \"Posting Blocked (UAT-04)\" to \"Entry Blocked \342\200\224 Out of Balance.\" I\'m also revising terms in the Table Footer, Invariant banner, and Quote & Invoice Lines tab for greater clarity. I\'m focusing on ensuring these elements accurately communicate their business meaning to non-technical users. For instance, `SUM(debit_base) = SUM(credit_base) \302\267 Immutable posted entry` is now translated to `Debits equal credits \302\267 Permanently locked once posted`. Finally, I am looking into error messages related to imbalanced entries.\n\n\n**Refining Workflow Mapping**\n\nI\'m now diving into the Connected Workflow Section, ensuring it\'s clear and business-focused. I\'m removing technical jargon and internal codes like `J-01` and references to `CRM-01`, aiming for user-friendly descriptions. I\'m replacing technical terms such as `state machine` with accessible language like `approvals` and `business safeguards`. I\'ve also identified potential instances of technical jargon (like `debit_base`) to review. I\'ll briefly inspect `packages/contracts/src/erp.ts` and handle component display, perhaps in `HeroSection.tsx`, so as not to cause unit test failures.\n\n\n**Simplifying Technical Detail**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the Selected Stage Detail panel, striving for clarity and business-focused descriptions. I\'m removing all internal codes and technical jargon. The goal is to declutter and create a simplified view, specifically by transforming the technical cards into clear, user-friendly business cards. I\'m working on reframing each of the four cards to represent a straightforward business-focused view. For example, I\'m replacing technical terms such as `API Endpoint` and `Outbox Event Key`, with business concepts.\n\n\n**Enhancing User Understanding**\n\nI\'m now revising the API Endpoint and Outbox Event Key terminology to be much clearer. For instance, I\'m changing *\"Convert qualified prospect into a unified customer record\"* and *\"Creates a verified customer profile with full lead history\"* to be more user-focused. This will enhance understanding for non-technical users. I\'ve also focused on the Modules Grid Section, and simplified internal codes for clarity.\n\n\n**Refining Module Presentation**\n\nI\'m now revising the Modules Grid. I\'ve simplified the filter options to `All Modules`, `Core`, `Operations`, and `Expansion`. I\'m replacing technical details with business-friendly labels, focusing on categories and capabilities. I\'m eliminating schema names and internal codes (`CORE-01`), and focusing on clear titles and summaries, for example, `Company, Access & Contacts` or `6 core capabilities` .\n\n\n**Improving Module Clarity**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the Module Grid and Detail Drawer for improved user experience. I\'m excited about how I can now show `{mod.keyFeatures.length} key capabilities` and `Explore module \342\206\227`, replacing clunky technical clutter. I\'ve eliminated `tables`, `P0/P1/P2`, and all those internal codes like `CRM-01/SAL-01/FIN-01`. Now, in the Detail Drawer, I\'m revising the eyebrow text and replacing terms like `Aggregate Ownership` with user-focused descriptions such as `What This Module Manages`. I\'ve also swapped those technical panels for clear, business-focused views such as `Included Records & Tools`.\n\n\n**Revising Trust Element**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the Trust, Security & Reliability section. I\'m focusing on clarifying the \"Built for financial accuracy, strict privacy, and audit readiness\" message. I\'m making sure to simplify the technical jargon of frameworks (e.g., NestJS, Next.js, and PostgreSQL), and replacing it with business-focused language, such as METR keeping operations and books in one, tightly governed platform. I am working on the 6-card metrics strip and the layout, so as not to break existing component tests.\n\n\n**Defining Assurance Showcases**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the Governance & Assurance Showcase, specifically the four interactive tabs. I\'m replacing the technical details like SQL DDL and JSON API payloads with business-focused visual cards. For instance, in \"Financial Safeguards\", I\'m transforming the comparison checklist to highlight how METR prevents accounting errors. The goal is to provide a clear and user-friendly experience, demonstrating METR\'s robust features.\n\n\n**Clarifying Reliability Metrics**\n\nI\'m now revising the Reliability & Security metrics with an eye toward user-friendliness. I\'m focusing on the bottom 6-card metrics strip and replacing the internal codes (e.g., `NFR-01`) and technical jargon. My goal is to clearly communicate platform reliability and security commitments to a broader audience. I am replacing technical metrics with plain language explanations. For example, I\'m translating `NFR-01` to `Platform Uptime: 99.5%+ Continuously monitored availability`. I am also working on `Everyday Speed` metric and ensuring that the section meets our compliance goals. I aim to ensure there is an easier understanding of what users get.\n\n\n**Refining Roadmap Presentation**\n\nI\'m now revising the Roadmap Section for better user clarity, focusing on the release milestones. I\'ve switched the view from \"Release Milestones (M0\342\200\223M5)\" to \"Rollout Stages\" and \"Vertical Slices (0\342\200\2237)\" to \"Step-by-Step Timeline\". I\'m also replacing codes such as `M0` through `M5` with step numbers like `01` through `06`, aiming for straightforward communication, and simplifying the eyebrow, headline, and subtitle to align with user understanding.\n\n\n**Simplifying Roadmap Detail**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the Roadmap section, focusing on simplifying technical details within the milestones. I\'m focusing on translating technical language (like `OIDC`, `composite (tenant_id, id) foreign keys`, `outbox pipelines`, `PITR`, `UAT`, `OWASP ASVS L2`, and `concurrency reservation conflict tests`) into straightforward business language. I\'m also revising titles and headers, like changing \"Engineering Deliverables\" to \"What You Get,\" and replacing \"Business Review Evidence\" with \"How You\'ll See It Working.\" I\'m also changing \"Quality Exit Gate\" to \"Go-Live Readiness Check.\" I am translating the step-by-step timeline, as well as replacing all the internal codes from earlier, such as `Slice 00` through `Slice 07` and cleaning all the jargon, such as translating `Repository, environments, IAM, CI, migration discipline` to `Workspace setup, secure sign-in, multi-company structure, and automated backups`.\n\n**Refining FAQ Content**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the FAQ Section. I am changing the eyebrow text to read \"Frequently Asked Questions,\" and refining the headline. I am working to keep it clear and business-focused. I am concentrating on user understanding, and making the section easier to understand.\n\n\n**Enhancing FAQ Clarity**\n\nI\'m now revising the FAQ section, specifically by refining titles and descriptions. I\'m focusing on user understanding and removing technical references. I\'m streamlining the categories, eliminating internal references (`METR-SPEC-001`), and rewriting the Q&A in clear, business-focused language, such as replacing \"modular monolith vs microservices\" with \"Why use one unified platform instead of stitching separate apps together?\"\n\n\n**Improving Waitlist Messaging**\n\nI\'m now revising the Waitlist section to enhance user clarity and focus. I\'m streamlining the bullet points and labels for better readability. I\'m focusing on transforming technical jargon into user-friendly business descriptions, and I\'m updating terms to align with business-focused language, particularly regarding the onboarding process. For instance, `Dedicated onboarding plan tailored to your team and entities` replaces technical references and clarifies the purpose of the onboarding walkthrough. I\'m also ensuring the language is concise and easily understood by all users.\n\n\n**Revising Waitlist Elements**\n\nI\'m now revising the waitlist submission for clarity and conciseness, especially around the onboarding process. I am replacing \"Footprint\" with \"Company Structure,\" simplifying \"Priority Rollout Scope\" to \"Modules You\'re Most Interested In,\" and refining \"Phase 1\" description to focus on core business functions: Company Setup, CRM, Quotes & Orders, Invoicing, and Accounting. I have also simplified references to Legacy system information and technical jargon, which aligns with my focus on user experience. I\'m also ensuring all the error messages and success messages are rewritten in user-friendly language.\n\n\n\n"
